<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">University of Delaware</span>, <span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_city">Newark</span>, <span id="id7" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_state">Delaware</span>, <span id="id8" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_country">USA</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">2025</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has recently shown that large language models (LLMs) can develop their own reasoning without direct supervision. However, applications in the medical domain, specifically for question answering, are susceptible to significant reward hacking during the reasoning phase. Our work addresses two primary forms of this behavior: i) providing a final answer without preceding reasoning, and ii) employing non-standard reasoning formats to exploit the reward mechanism. To mitigate these, we introduce a composite reward function with specific penalties for these behaviors. Our experiments show that extending RLVR with our proposed reward model leads to better-formatted reasoning with less reward hacking and good accuracy compared to the baselines. This approach marks a step toward reducing reward hacking and enhancing the reliability of models utilizing RLVR<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        The codebase for our project is available at <a href="https://github.com/healthylaife/Composite-LLM-Reward-Model" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/healthylaife/Composite-LLM-Reward-Model</a></span></span></span>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Reinforcement Learning, Reward Hacking, Large Language Models
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) are demonstrating great skill in answering complex medical questions and assisting with diagnoses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singhal et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. However, a fundamental challenge that is faced by researchers is to make the decision-making process accurate, reliable, and trustworthy. This question has driven researchers toward reinforcement learning (RL) approaches that go beyond traditional pre-training and supervised fine-tuning. Training language models with RL enables optimization on complex, sequence-level objectives that are not easily differentiable and, therefore, not well-suited for traditional supervised fine-tuning (SFT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This has led to the development of various RL paradigms for LLM training. Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) has become perhaps the most well-known approach, where human evaluators provide preference signals to train reward models that capture human judgment about response quality, helpfulness, and safety. Models like ChatGPT and Claude have been shaped through RLHF to be more helpful and less likely to produce harmful outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. However, RLHF faces significant scalability challenges—human evaluation is expensive, time-consuming, and can be inconsistent across different evaluators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, Reinforcement Learning from AI Feedback (RLAIF) has emerged as an alternative, where AI systems themselves provide the feedback signals, potentially offering more consistent and scalable evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Yet both RLHF and RLAIF primarily focus on the final outputs of models rather than the reasoning processes that lead to those outputs. This creates a critical gap in domains like healthcare, where understanding how a model arrived at its conclusion is often as important as the conclusion itself.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this context, Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has emerged as a promising methodology to enhance the transparency and reliability of model reasoning. In this framework, a model receives reward signals based on the correctness of its final answer as verified against ground truth. The central hypothesis is that rewarding correct outcomes encourages the model to generate coherent and valid reasoning paths that lead to those outcomes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton et al., 1999</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">However, it has been observed that the models can attempt to bypass the instruction for response generation and achieve rewards through gaming or reward-hacking behavior. This phenomenon, often referred to as “specification gaming” or “reward hacking,” occurs when an AI system finds unexpected ways to maximize its reward function that technically satisfy the specified objective but violate the spirit of what the designers intended <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krakovna et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. In the context of language models, this manifests as sophisticated strategies that superficially satisfy correctness but fundamentally undermine interpretability. Models may exploit subtle statistical patterns to reverse-engineer correct answers, then generate post-hoc justifications that appear logical but are essentially fabricated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turpin et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This behavior has been documented across domains from mathematical reasoning to reading comprehension, where models learn to pattern-match rather than develop genuine understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, and has also been observed in biomedical applications, such as medical question answering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For example, suppose a model is instructed to present the reasoning and the correct answer in a specific format in medical question-answering tasks. In that case, the model might attempt to gain rewards by prematurely revealing the answer without proper reasoning or presenting the reasoning in a format that does not comply with the instructions. However, there are no universal solutions to eliminate such reward hacking behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose a composite reward model to mitigate reward hacking in LLMs, especially targeting medical question-answering tasks when using RLVR. In particular, our contributions are:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have designed a composite reward model that incorporates multiple penalty functions to detect whether the model is manipulating the reward by generating answers within the thinking step or producing longer, step-by-step reasoning (without following the given instructions) and mitigating these behaviors.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the binary reward function, <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{binary}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>binary</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{binary}}</annotation></semantics></math>, from RLVR, we introduce two new penalty functions: <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{answer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{answer}}</annotation></semantics></math> to penalize the model for outputting the answer directly without any reasoning, and <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{structural}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>structural</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{structural}}</annotation></semantics></math> for violating the instructions on response generation. Our goal was to create transparent, interpretable, and lightweight mechanisms that could be easily adapted or extended.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method in both in-distribution and out-of-distribution scenarios, in addition to using human judges and LLM judges for verification.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Literature Review</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs have shown broad abilities in generating text and engaging in conversation, supported by their size and pattern recognition from extensive datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. However, ensuring that outputs are accurate and match human values has required sophisticated training approaches and evaluation mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poulain et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) represented a significant advancement, incorporating human preference data into reward systems to refine models for usefulness and safety <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While successful, RLHF requires substantial computational resources and varies due to inconsistencies in human judgment. Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) addresses these constraints by rewarding outcomes that can be objectively verified, proving particularly effective in domains such as mathematics and programming, where correctness can be easily confirmed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. The underlying principle is that rewarding verifiable results promotes more dependable reasoning processes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, single reward mechanisms often prove inadequate, leading to the adoption of composite reward functions. These combine correctness incentives with penalties that discourage harmful, overly verbose, or biased outputs. Constitutional approaches illustrate this framework by employing predefined principles to guide model behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Our method shares conceptual similarities but differs in focus: while Constitutional approaches emphasize broad ethical alignment, we develop specific penalties for reward exploitation in clinical reasoning. Unlike iterative refinement methods, our penalties are rule-based and interpretable, enabling immediate identification of why penalties were applied. Finally, composite reward methods have been examined in fields such as finance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Srivastava et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; their application to medical reasoning remains underdeveloped.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, a major challenge is that models can exploit reward definitions—a phenomenon termed specification gaming or reward hacking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark and Amodei, 2016</a>)</cite>. In language models, this can occur when a system produces correct final answers but with nonsensical reasoning, or when it engages in excessive agreement-seeking behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Med-RLVR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> identified such vulnerabilities but offered no solutions. Our method extends it by combining correctness rewards with penalties for answer disclosure and structural violations, directly addressing the gaming behaviors observed in Med-RLVR.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Observed reward hacking behavior in medical question answering. In the first response, the model generated the answer directly inside the reasoning tag (<span id="S2.T1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;&lt;/think&gt;</span>) to achieve a reward without any actual reasoning. In the second example, the model attempts to achieve a reward by generating a step-by-step reasoning outside the <span id="S2.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span> tags, which contradicts the given instruction.</figcaption>
<div id="S2.T1.6" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:73.4pt;vertical-align:-35.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-254.2pt,54.1pt) scale(0.404293139848907,0.404293139848907) ;">
<table id="S2.T1.6.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S2.T1.6.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S2.T1.6.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example Question</span></th>
<th id="S2.T1.6.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt"><span id="S2.T1.6.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example Response</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<table id="S2.T1.6.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><span id="S2.T1.6.1.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct answer</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><span id="S2.T1.6.1.2.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">revelation</span></td></tr>
</table></td>
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<table id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">A 35-year-old man comes to the physician because of itchy,</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">watery eyes for the past week…</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment?</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">A: Erythromycin ointment;</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">B: Ketotifen eye drops;</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">C: Warm compresses;</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">D: Fluorometholone eye ;drops</td></tr>
</table></td>
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<table id="S2.T1.6.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">&lt;think&gt;The most appropriate treatment for the patient’s</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2.3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">symptoms is Ketotifen eye drops. &lt;/think&gt;</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.2.3.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.2.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">&lt;answer&gt;B &lt;/answer&gt;</td></tr>
</table></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<table id="S2.T1.6.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><span id="S2.T1.6.1.3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structural non</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left"><span id="S2.T1.6.1.3.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">compliance</span></td></tr>
</table></td>
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<table id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">A 42-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">of a 2-day history of right upper abdominal pain and nausea. …</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient’s symptoms?</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">A: Autodigestion of pancreatic parenchyma;</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">B: Hypomotility of the gallbladder;</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">C: Fistula between the gallbladder and small intestine;</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">D: Infection with a hepatotropic virus;</td></tr>
</table></td>
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<table id="S2.T1.6.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">To solve this problem, let’s go through each option step-by-step and evaluate its likelihood</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">based on the given information… Based on the reasoning process,</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.3.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">the most likely cause of the patient’s symptoms is</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.6.1.3.3.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.6.1.3.3.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">&lt;think&gt;Obstruction of the cystic duct &lt;/think&gt;&lt;answer&gt;E &lt;/answer&gt;</td></tr>
</table></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will cover some preliminary topics on which our method is based.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR)</span>
is a novel method for training language models on tasks with verifiable outcomes, such as mathematical problem-solving and instruction following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, as well as in medical MCQ answering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. RLVR leverages the existing RLHF objective but replaces the reward model with a verification function. RLVR is based on a simple principle, common in RL literature, applied to language models: the policy only receives a reward when its generated responses are verifiably correct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In our project, to connect the verifiable reward model to the LLM policy, we have utilized the REINFORCE method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton et al., 1999</a>)</cite></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">REINFORCE</span>
enables credit assignment, telling the model how to adjust its parameters so that the probability of generating high-reward (verifiably good) responses increases. This policy gradient method updates the parameters of a stochastic policy by maximizing the expected reward. Formally, REINFORCE estimates the gradient of the expected return as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}J(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\theta}}\left[\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|s)\cdot(R-b(s))\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}J(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\theta}}\left[\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(a|s)\cdot(R-b(s))\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the generated action (response), <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the input prompt, <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is the total reward, and <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned baseline used to reduce variance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This framework allows the model to learn from sparse and delayed reward signals while incorporating structure-aware penalties through our composite reward.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The work by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a></cite> showed that reward hacking can manifest in two ways when using RLVR in medical question answering: direct answer revelation and structural non-compliance (an example is provided in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2. Literature Review ‣ Reward Hacking Mitigation using Verifiable Composite Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). To elaborate, as we are giving the model reward for the correct answer provided in a specific format (between <span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;answer&gt;&lt;/answer&gt;</span> tags), it tries to gain rewards by revealing the answer early in the reasoning step (first example in the Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2. Literature Review ‣ Reward Hacking Mitigation using Verifiable Composite Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and without detailed reasoning (direct or premature answer revelation) and by putting the reasoning outside of specified tag (e.g. <span id="S3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;&lt;/think&gt;</span>) (we call it structural non-compliance which is the second example in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2. Literature Review ‣ Reward Hacking Mitigation using Verifiable Composite Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate the highlighted issues, we employ reward shaping through a composite reward function. The proposed method is shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4. Method ‣ Reward Hacking Mitigation using Verifiable Composite Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. It involves generating structured responses to medical questions and evaluating them with a combination of correctness and format-based rewards and penalties. A baseline network is used to stabilize learning, and a policy gradient method is employed to optimize the model. The full details of the reward structure, training process, and optimization strategy are provided in the following sections.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15557v1/main_diag.svg" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/222;" width="381" height="222"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>Training of an LLM with our proposed Composite Reward Model</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Composite Reward Function</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The composite reward function, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> combines a primary task reward, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{binary}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>binary</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{binary}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is given for the correct answer in the proper format, with two distinct heuristic-based penalty functions designed to penalize the above-mentioned hacking scenarios, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{answer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{answer}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{structural}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>structural</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{structural}}</annotation></semantics></math>. These heuristics were designed based on empirically observed failure modes (as illustrated in Table 1) and allowed rapid deployment without the need for labeled training data.
. This aligns with the common practice of using targeted reward components to fine-tune large language models across multiple behavioral axes simultaneously <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> represent a single generation produced by the model in response to a given prompt. Our goal is to define a composite reward function, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{total}}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{total}}(g)</annotation></semantics></math>, that accurately scores this generation. This function is a weighted combination of the listed distinct components and is formulated as:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R_{\text{total}}(g)=w_{b}R_{\text{binary}}(g)-w_{a}P_{\text{answer}}(g)-w_{s}P_{\text{structural}}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>binary</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>structural</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R_{\text{total}}(g)=w_{b}R_{\text{binary}}(g)-w_{a}P_{\text{answer}}(g)-w_{s}P_{\text{structural}}(g)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{b},w_{a},w_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{b},w_{a},w_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> are the positive, real-valued weights assigned to the binary reward, direct answer penalty, and structural penalty, respectively. These weights are hyperparameters that are tuned to balance the importance of each component. We will define each component in detail next.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Binary Reward (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\text{binary}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">binary</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\text{binary}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our method, we have used binary rewards as continuous rewards can introduce their instabilities, including reward hacking where models exploit smooth gradients to achieve high scores without genuine improvement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. OpenAI’s process reward models also use discrete verification signals for mathematical reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. It provides the primary signal for the completion of the task (generating the correct response to the Multiple-Choice Question Answering (MCQA)).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the final answer choice extracted from generation <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the correct ground truth answer from the MCQA label. The binary reward is an indicator function that returns <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> for a correct answer and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> for an incorrect answer. If the model outputs an answer that is both incorrect and the format is similar to the reward hacking examples shown in table-<a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2. Literature Review ‣ Reward Hacking Mitigation using Verifiable Composite Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which are wrong answer formats, then the reward is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R_{\text{binary}}(g)=\begin{cases}+1&amp;\text{if }a_{g}=a^{*}\text{and correct format}\\
0&amp;\text{if }a_{g}\neq a^{*}\text{and correct format}\\
-1&amp;\text{if }a_{g}\neq a^{*}\text{ and wrong format}\\
\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>binary</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and correct format</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and correct format</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and wrong format</mtext></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R_{\text{binary}}(g)=\begin{cases}+1&amp;\text{if }a_{g}=a^{*}\text{and correct format}\\
0&amp;\text{if }a_{g}\neq a^{*}\text{and correct format}\\
-1&amp;\text{if }a_{g}\neq a^{*}\text{ and wrong format}\\
\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2. </span><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Penalty for "Premature Answer Revelation" (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{answer}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">answer</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{answer}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In clinical MCQ answering tasks, it has been observed that the model learning to parrot answer-like phrases within the reasoning block or directly provide the answer in the reasoning block to shortcut the reasoning process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. To mitigate this, we implemented a semantic penalty that utilizes semantic similarity to detect premature answer revelation. Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{think}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>think</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{think}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the text content extracted from the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span> block of the generation <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The core of this method is an embedding function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:\mathcal{T}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:\mathcal{T}\to\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which maps a text string from the space of all texts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to a dense vector of dimensions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. We use a pre-trained Sentence Transformer model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reimers and Gurevych, 2019</a>)</cite> for this mapping.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The penalty is calculated through a multiple-step process. First, a set of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> prototypical answer leak phrases are defined , shown as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\{l_{1},l_{2},\ldots,l_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\{l_{1},l_{2},\ldots,l_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. A leak phrase is a phrase that directly says what the answer is without any reasoning inside the think tag. Example phrases include "the correct answer is", "the answer is definitely", "the choice is ", "Option C is the right one", "We can conclude the answer is", and " the solution is B".
After that, the vector embeddings for this set are precomputed: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{L}=\{E(l_{1}),E(l_{2}),\ldots,E(l_{m})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{L}=\{E(l_{1}),E(l_{2}),\ldots,E(l_{m})\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For generation <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the embedding of its thinking block: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{think}}=E(t_{\text{think}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>think</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>think</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{think}}=E(t_{\text{think}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we find the maximum cosine similarity between <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{think}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>think</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{think}}</annotation></semantics></math> and any vector in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we calculate <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\text{answer}}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\text{answer}}(g)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the maximum of these similarities. The final penalty is applied only if this score exceeds a predefined threshold <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{answer}}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{answer}}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The magnitude of the penalty is the score itself, which is proportional to the certainty of the answer leak.</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S_{\text{answer}}(g)=\max_{i=1,\ldots,m}\left(\text{Sim}(v_{\text{think}},E(l_{i}))\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mtext>Sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>think</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S_{\text{answer}}(g)=\max_{i=1,\ldots,m}\left(\text{Sim}(v_{\text{think}},E(l_{i}))\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{\text{answer}}(g)=\begin{cases}S_{\text{answer}}(g)&amp;\text{if }S_{\text{answer}}(g)&gt;\tau_{\text{answer}}\\
0.0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>answer</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0.0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{\text{answer}}(g)=\begin{cases}S_{\text{answer}}(g)&amp;\text{if }S_{\text{answer}}(g)&gt;\tau_{\text{answer}}\\
0.0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3. </span><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Penalty for Structural Non-Compliance(<math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{structural}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">structural</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{structural}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To discourage the LLM from generating responses where there are too much text or reasoning outside of the think tags, we incorporate a structural penalty exploit <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{structural}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>structural</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{structural}}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the word count preceding the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span> tag. Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{preamble}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{preamble}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the textual content generated before the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span> tag. The penalty is applied if the number of words in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{preamble}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{preamble}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T_{preamble}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T_{preamble}|</annotation></semantics></math>, exceeds a predefined threshold <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{preamble}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{preamble}</annotation></semantics></math>. A fixed penalty <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied in such cases:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{\text{structural}}(g)=\begin{cases}\lambda_{s}&amp;\text{if }T_{preamble}&gt;\tau\\
0&amp;otherwise\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>structural</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{\text{structural}}(g)=\begin{cases}\lambda_{s}&amp;\text{if }T_{preamble}&gt;\tau\\
0&amp;otherwise\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This component ensures that the model avoids inserting unnecessary preambles or distracting content outside the clinical reasoning section, promoting compact and structured responses.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To optimize the model for this reward signal, we apply a policy gradient method, the REINFORCE algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton et al., 1999</a>)</cite>. In our method, the LLM parameterizes <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and responses are sampled via auto-regressive generation. The log probabilities <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\pi_{\theta}(a|s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\pi_{\theta}(a|s)</annotation></semantics></math> of the generated tokens are computed, and the total reward <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated using a composite reward function that accounts for correctness and penalties (e.g., for hacking or exploitation). Then the baseline <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is estimated using a learned neural network that takes as input the average hidden state of the final generated tokens. The advantage and policy loss are computed as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A=R-b(s)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A=R-b(s),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{policy}}=-\sum_{t=1}^{T}\log\pi_{\theta}(a_{t}|a_{&lt;t},s)\cdot A," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>policy</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{policy}}=-\sum_{t=1}^{T}\log\pi_{\theta}(a_{t}|a_{&lt;t},s)\cdot A,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of the generated sequence. Additionally, when using a baseline network, a mean squared error loss is used to train it:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{baseline}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{2}\left(b(s_{i})-R_{i}\right)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>baseline</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{baseline}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{1}{2}\left(b(s_{i})-R_{i}\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The total loss used for backpropagation is then <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{policy}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{baseline}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>policy</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>baseline</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{policy}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{baseline}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This setup ensures that the policy learns to generate higher-reward outputs while reducing variance through the use of baseline estimation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have used two open-source LLMs for our experiments: Llama 3.2-3B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024</a>)</cite>. The experiments were run on Amazon AWS Sagemaker Studio with ml.g5.4xlarge and ml.p3.8xlarge instances.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For training, we utilize the MedQA-USMLE-4-options dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, which comprises multiple-choice questions (with four options) derived from professional medical board exams and covers a broad range of medical topics that require domain-specific knowledge and reasoning skills (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Baselines ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Reward Hacking Mitigation using Verifiable Composite Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). At first, we train the LLM with RLVR and our proposed composite reward model for one epoch (due to resource limitations) with 1,000 samples from the dataset. The hyperparameters for the reward model were chosen as <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{b}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{b}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{a}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{a}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During testing, we utilize in-distribution data from the same dataset. For experiments involving out-of-distribution data, we employ the health subset of the MMLU-PRO benchmark dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to assess generalizability. For testing, we have used 200 data samples from each dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the baseline, we use the non-fine-tuned version of the Llama3.2-3B-instruct model and the Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct model. In addition to the baselines, we utilize the supervised fine-tuned versions of Llama (Llama3.2-3B SFT (CoT)) and Qwen (Qwen2.5-3B SFT (CoT)), which incorporate RLVR but do not implement our proposed reward model. The supervised fine-tuning (SFT) approach here transforms the dataset into a conversational format. Then each medical question is converted into a structured prompt that asks the model to provide step-by-step reasoning within <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span> tags, followed by the final answer choice in <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;answer&gt;</span> tags. The model is then fine-tuned using LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) with 4-bit quantization for memory efficiency, training on these formatted examples to learn both the reasoning process and the specific response format. This approach teaches the model to generate chain-of-thought (CoT) explanations for medical questions while maintaining the required structured output format, essentially conditioning the model to think through clinical scenarios systematically before providing answers. Finally, the LLMs which implement our reward model are designated as <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama3.2-3B SFT (CoT) + RM </span> and <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qwen2.5-3B SFT (CoT) + RM</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>An example question from the MedQA-USMLE dataset</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:327.8pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question:</span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-bottom: 5.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:327.8pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">A 3-month-old baby died suddenly at night while asleep. His mother noticed that he had died only after she awoke in the morning. No cause of death was determined based on the autopsy. Which of the following precautions could have prevented the death of the baby?</span>
