</section>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the models based on the accuracy of their answers to the questions, as well as their ability to follow instructions without exhibiting gaming behavior. We measure this by calculating the format violation rate. To detect format violations or hacking, the model calculates the semantic similarity between the reasoning in the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span> tag and a predefined list of answer-leaking phrases (e.g., "the correct answer is"). For this, we use SBERT or Sentence BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reimers and Gurevych, 2019</a>)</cite>. When excessive text appears before the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;think&gt;</span> tag—specifically, if the word count exceeds a set limit, a fixed penalty is also assigned. We then calculate how many times these are happening in the responses to calculate the format violation or hacking rate.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Performance Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results of the initial experiments are shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Prompts ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ Reward Hacking Mitigation using Verifiable Composite Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. On the MedQA-USMLE-4-Option dataset, the Llama3.2-3B SFT (CoT) + RM approach achieved an accuracy of 0.42 <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.09, representing a modest improvement over the baseline Llama3.2-3B model (0.41 <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.02) and the standard SFT approach (0.41 <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.05). Interestingly, the Llama3.2-3B SFT (CoT) + RM approach achieves the 2nd-highest hacking rate after the baseline for this particular dataset. However, on the out-of-distribution MMLU-PRO-Health dataset, the Llama3.2-3B SFT (CoT) + RM achieved the lowest accuracy and highest hacking rate compared to the other 2 models. We believe this is more so due to the knowledge required to answer the questions from the MMLU-Pro-Health dataset, which covers a broader range of topics related to biology and health compared to the MedQA dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the Qwen2.5-3B model family, the proposed method showed great performance. On MedQA-USMLE, the Qwen2.5-3B SFT (CoT) + RM approach achieved an accuracy of 0.4 <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.3 and hacking rate of 0.05 <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.2, which outperformed the baseline (0.60 <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.48) and standard SFT (0.23 <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.42). In the out-of-distribution setting, Qwen2.5-3B SFT (CoT) + RM achieved the lowest hacking rate (0.2 <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.31).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>Evaluation with Varying Penalty Thresholds</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have experimented on the test dataset with semantic similarity ranging from 0.5 to 1.5 and preamble length ranging from 5 to 50. We show in table-<a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.2. Evaluation with Varying Penalty Thresholds ‣ 6. Results ‣ Reward Hacking Mitigation using Verifiable Composite Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> that with our model, for different lengths, we get a lower reward hacking rate compared to not using the model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4. </span>Here we are showing the answer violation rate and structural violation rate for different values of <math id="S6.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{answer}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{answer}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{preamble}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{preamble}</annotation></semantics></math> with and without the implementation of our proposed composite reward model. We can observe that, using our reward model, the overall violation rate is reduced.</figcaption>
<div id="S6.T4.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:111.8pt;vertical-align:-54.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-76.9pt,24.9pt) scale(0.691729365633895,0.691729365633895) ;">
<table id="S6.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
