<td id="A2.T6.2.1.6.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">15</td>
<td id="A2.T6.2.1.6.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">15</td>
<td id="A2.T6.2.1.6.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">150</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted an online survey with 15 participants who had no clinical expertise but held a college-level degree. Each participant was provided 10 pairs of responses generated from the non-finetuned LLM and the fine-tuned LLM (with our reward model). The participants responded by saying which response better followed the format (&lt;think&gt;reasoning&lt;/think&gt; and &lt;answer&gt;answer choice&lt;/answer&gt;). Their responses are provided in table-<a href="#A2.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix B Human Feedback Survey on Quality of the Generated Responses ‣ Reward Hacking Mitigation using Verifiable Composite Rewards" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
